      "title": "mal — bien finally gets its opposite",
      "headword": "mal",
      "romanization": "mal",
      "gloss": "badly — the partner bien has been waiting for since the opening pages, in a long form and a short one",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:3d995ce48f1e732c",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Bien has been with you since chapter one, and so has bueno."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Both have an opposite, and the pair works exactly the same way."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart: mal",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Malo is the adjective — bad. Mal is what is left of it when it goes in front of a masculine noun, and it is also the adverb — badly."
            },
            {
              "kind": "table",
              "headers": [
                "",
                ""
              ],
              "columns": 2,
              "rowCount": 2,
              "utterances": [
                "bien / mal, well / badly.",
                "bueno / malo, good / bad."
              ]
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Estoy mal. — I'm not well."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Both come straight from Latin: bene and malus, which you can still hear in English malady and malice."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "bien, then mal",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *bien*, then *mal*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "bueno, then malo",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *bueno*, then *malo*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "repeat",
              "times": 2,
              "source": "[REPEAT x2]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C60-mal-say",
              "prompt": "Somebody asks how you are, and you are not well. Answer.",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-LEX-MAL"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 12,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "estoy mal",
                "mal",
                "estoy mal."
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "The adverb, opposite of bien.",
                "incorrect": "Estoy mal."
              }
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C60-mal-adj",
              "prompt": "Which form is the plain adjective, bad -- mal or malo?",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-LEX-MALO"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 12,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "malo",
                "malo, the long form"
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "Long when it stands on its own.",
                "incorrect": "Malo."
              }
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C60-mal-pairs",
              "prompt": "Give the opposite of bien, and the opposite of bueno.",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-LEX-BIEN",
                "ES-MORPH-BUENO-BUENA",
                "ES-LEX-MAL"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 14,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "mal and malo",
                "mal, malo",
                "mal / malo"
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "Adverb against adverb, adjective against adjective.",
                "incorrect": "Bien becomes mal; bueno becomes malo."
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "How do you say you are not well? (Estoy mal.)"
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "And which English words still carry Latin malus? (Malady, malice.) Next: the short form and the long form turn out to be a habit, not a quirk."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C60-apocope",
      "sequence": 2377,
      "title": "a word in a hurry — the short form and the long one",
      "headword": "mucho · muy, malo · mal",
      "romanization": "mucho · muy, malo · mal",
      "gloss": "the same biting-short seen twice, which makes it a habit of the language rather than a pair of quirks",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:de5b4f267940d893",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Muy — very. Mal — badly."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Each of those arrived beside a longer word, and the pairing was not a coincidence either time."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "culture-pragmatics",
          "title": "Why it's said this way",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Set the two pairs beside each other."
            },
            {
              "kind": "table",
              "headers": [
                "long form",
                "short form",
                "short one appears"
              ],
              "columns": 3,
              "rowCount": 2,
              "utterances": [
                "long form: mucho. short form: muy. short one appears: before an adjective or adverb.",
                "long form: malo. short form: mal. short one appears: before a masculine noun, and as the adverb."
              ]
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "This is not two exceptions. It is one habit of the language: Spanish bites the ending off a word that is leaning on another word, and keeps it whole when that word stands on its own."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Once you have heard it twice you start noticing it everywhere, and it stops being something to memorise. A short form is a word in a hurry to get to the next one."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "mucho / muy, then malo / mal, and hear the same trick twice",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *mucho* / *muy*, then *malo* / *mal*, and hear the same trick twice]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "repeat",
              "times": 2,
              "source": "[REPEAT x2]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C60-apocope-pattern",
              "prompt": "What do mucho/muy and malo/mal have in common?",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-GRAMMAR-APOCOPE-PATTERN"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 16,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "the ending is bitten off when it leans on another word",
                "a long form and a short form",
                "apocope"
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "One habit, not two exceptions.",
                "incorrect": "Each has a long form and a short one, and the short one leans on the next word."
              }
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C60-apocope-which",
              "prompt": "Which of each pair stands on its own -- mucho or muy, malo or mal?",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-LEX-MUY",
                "ES-LEX-MALO"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 14,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "mucho and malo",
                "mucho, malo",
                "the long forms"
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "Whole when alone, short when leaning.",
                "incorrect": "The long forms: mucho and malo."
              }
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C60-apocope-hear",
              "prompt": "When a Spanish word loses its ending, what is it about to do?",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-GRAMMAR-APOCOPE-PATTERN",
                "ES-LEX-MAL"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 14,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "lean on the word after it",
                "hurry to the next word",
                "attach to the following word"
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "The ending goes where the word is in a hurry.",
                "incorrect": "It is about to lean on the word that follows."
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "When does Spanish bite the ending off? (When the word is leaning on another word.)"
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "And what was muy before it was in a hurry? (Latin multum.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
